<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>

  <!-- One feed, named, in the repo.

       Gotcha found the hard way while writing this file: NuGet reads it as
       XML, and an XML comment may not contain two hyphens in a row. A comment
       quoting the command line flags of a dotnet tool install broke every
      restore in the repo with "NuGet.Config is not valid XML ... An XML
      comment cannot contain two hyphens" (measured on SDK 8.0.427). Commands
      are named here without their flags for that reason.

       NuGet reads configuration in layers: a machine level config, a user
       level config, and the nearest file in the directory tree. Without this
       file, the feeds a build resolves against are whatever that machine
       happens to have, and nothing in the repo says which. The clear element
       drops the inherited set so the only source in play is the one named
       below.

       Why a single named feed matters for this project specifically:

         - The GitVersion tool the release job installs (release.yml) is a
           downloaded package that is then executed. It sits outside the project
           graph, so the committed packages.lock.json and the vulnerable package
           audit both never see it. A pinned version and a pinned feed are the
           only two controls over it.

         - PlayniteSDK and System.Text.Json are ordinary public names. NuGet
           answers a package request from the highest priority source that has
           it, and a name is not proof of who published it: an inherited source
           listed ahead of nuget.org can serve a package of the same id, at the
           pinned version or at a higher one, and the build cannot tell the
           difference from the name alone.

         - What catches the same id same version case is the lock file's sha512
           of the package content, checked on a locked restore (NU1403: "The
           package is different than the last restore"). That check only means
           something if the graph it records was resolved from a feed that was
           named, which is what this file does.

       The trade off, stated rather than hidden: a developer or a build agent
       that must restore through a mirror or an offline feed has to edit this
       file, and that edit is then a diff a reviewer sees. -->

  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" />
  </packageSources>

</configuration>
